# Intro
Uniform, metal-enriched gas in a periodic box that expands with the universe, cooling
radiatively with Grackle or geajay. The gas starts at 100 K, well above the temperature of the
cosmic microwave background (CMB), T_CMB(z) = T_CMB,0 (1 + z). With the CMB temperature floor of
the cooling library on, the gas cools onto T_CMB(z) when the library follows the cooling
accurately over each time step. With the floor off, it cools far below T_CMB(z). The box holds
32768 gas particles at 10 H atoms/cm^3 and a metal mass fraction of 0.01, with no gravity and no
stars, and expands by a factor 1.25 in scale factor.

The floor is an option of the cooling library. In official Grackle it is
`GrackleCooling:cmb_temperature_floor` and T_CMB,0 is 2.73 K. In geajay it is
`GeajayCooling:metal_cooling_cmb_floor` and T_CMB,0 is 2.725 K. Both default to 1. They subtract
the metal cooling rate at T_CMB(z) from the metal cooling rate, so metal cooling stops at
T_CMB(z). `agora_cmb_temperature_floor` is a different option: SWIFT applies it itself as a
lower bound on the internal energy, with T_CMB,0 = 2.7255 K. This example leaves it at 0.

The time step decides the result with official Grackle. The floor holds only when the step is
short against the time the metal cooling needs to restore the floor. At the default
`n_steps=400` it does not hold; `n_steps=3200` does. geajay holds the floor at the default.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_1 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
Use `grackle_1` or a higher mode. With geajay, replace `--with-cooling=grackle_1` and
`--with-grackle=...` by `--with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay`.

# Run
```
redshift=9 cmb_floor=1 n_steps=3200 ./run.sh
redshift=9 cmb_floor=0 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_z<redshift>_floor<cmb_floor>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an
environment variable: `n_threads`, `redshift` (starting redshift), `cmb_floor`
(1 or 0), `gas_density` (atom/cm^3, physical), `gas_mass` (Msun), `temperature` (K),
`expansion` (a_end / a_begin), `n_steps`, `n_snapshots`, `swift_exe`, `run_dir` (a plain
directory name) and `extra_args` (further swift arguments). The metal mass fraction is in
`params.yml`. SWIFT rounds `n_steps` up to a power of two, so the default 400 gives 512 steps.
Keep `n_snapshots` a power of two (default 8), so that all snapshots fall at the same point of
a time step. A run takes a few minutes on 8 threads at the default step count.

# Check
```
python3 checkCMBFloor.py run_z9_floor1
```
The snapshots hold no temperature, so the script rebuilds it from the internal energy, the
species and the metal mass fraction, as the cooling library does. It reads the library that ran
from `used_parameters.yml` and takes its T_CMB,0; `--t-cmb-0` overrides it. In every snapshot
after the first it checks that all temperatures are finite and that the lowest one is at least
T_CMB(z) within 1e-4 plus half the time step in ln a (floor held). The half step is an
empirical lag of the gas below the floor. In the last snapshot it checks that the highest
temperature is within 1e-3 above T_CMB(z) (floor reached). It exits with status 1 if a check
fails. A run with `cmb_floor=0` is expected to fail the floor-held check:

```
python3 checkCMBFloor.py run_z9_floor0 --expect-below-floor
```
exits with status 0 only if that run fails the floor-held check and all its temperatures end
below half of T_CMB(z).
